 {
  "source": "Goldman Sachs",
  "title": "H&R Block Inc. (HRB): F4Q reflects improved execution though structural risks remain",
  "type": "equity_research",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-08-12",
  "insights": [
   "HRB reported F4Q revenue of $1,145mn, up 3.0% y/y, beating consensus of 0.6% growth, driven by higher Net Average Charge (NAC) and a shift toward more complex tax filers.",
   "F4Q EBITDA margins contracted 50 bps y/y to 36.7%, but outperformed the 35.3% consensus estimate due to operating leverage and productivity gains.",
   "The company maintained assisted market share in F2026, marking a positive shift after years of losses, supported by a record 200 bps improvement in conversion and 190 bps in retention.",
   "HRB's F2027 guidance assumes maintained assisted share at the low end and share gains at the midpoint/high end, which may be optimistic given historical trends and limited room for further conversion normalization.",
   "Industry tax-return growth is expected to moderate below the historical ~1% rate due to slowing job growth, increasing HRB's reliance on pricing and mix to hit its 4-5% revenue growth target.",
   "In DIY, HRB is prioritizing paid conversion and complexity over lower-value volume, which may lead to further market share losses in F2027.",
   "HRB is testing a consultative model using AI and workflow automation to reduce manual tasks, with plans to expand this to a full designated market area in tax season 2027.",
   "Longer-term structural risks remain from AI-enabled DIY products that could provide personalized support and identify filing errors, potentially substituting for paid human assistance.",
   "Goldman Sachs reiterated a Sell rating and raised the 12-month price target from $29 to $33, reflecting an improved mix toward more complex filers."
  ],
  "market_movers": [
   {
    "ticker": "HRB",
    "action": "price_target_change",
    "rating": "Sell",
    "price_target": "$33.00",
    "rationale": "Reiterated Sell rating due to structural growth concerns and AI-related risks to the assisted tax preparation business, despite positive F4Q execution.",
    "conviction": "medium"
   }
  ],
  "earnings": [
